\title{CFPS-Sim: A Reproducible Starter Stack for Cell-Free Protein Synthesis}
\author{CFPS-Sim project}
\date{\today}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
Cell-free protein synthesis (CFPS) offers an experimentally accessible setting
for studying how formulation, sequence, and biochemical resources determine
protein production. CFPS-Sim is a reproducible computational scaffold for the
Fall 2026 How To Biomanufacture (Almost) Anything CFPS coopetition, situated in
the history of CFPS and the recent OpenAI--Ginkgo autonomous-laboratory study.
The project combines dispensing and concentration accounting, versioned DNA
constructs, a reduced Antimony/RoadRunner resource model with SBML export, and
a BoTorch demonstration of experiment selection. The three supplied plasmids
are checked against their source protein sequences, with GenBank import and
export supporting exchange with the Popcorn design toolkit. Construct-aware sfGFP simulation uses
sequence-derived nucleotide demands and twenty individual amino-acid pools;
transcript boundaries remain explicit assumptions. PETase and reteplase support
sequence accounting but require separate folding and activity observation models.

The article connects POSTECH's OT-2-assisted i-POPFLEX module-exchange experiments
to a proposed calibration and prospective comparison strategy, and reviews
measured improvements from CFPS active learning and Illinois biofoundry
optimization with their distinct targets and baselines. The proposed evaluation
separates assay calibration, identifiable effective-parameter fitting, and
prospective testing of experiment-selection policies under equal budgets.
It compares mechanistic information with an empirical baseline using withheld
recipes and batches, uncertainty coverage, and independently confirmed winners.
Python dependencies are locked with uv; execution is supported on Apple Silicon
CPUs and Modal. Seven software tests pass, and a sequence-aware Modal trajectory
agrees numerically with local execution. Tables and figures are generated with
Pweave and compiled into this article with Tectonic. Kinetics remain illustrative,
the optimizer uses synthetic observations, and no biological calibration or
competition improvement has yet been demonstrated.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction: from autonomous laboratories to the CFPS coopetition}
Cell-free protein synthesis (CFPS) produces proteins using biological machinery
outside living cells. Its experimental accessibility makes it a useful setting
for learning how recipe composition, resource availability, and measurement
affect biomanufacturing. The Fall 2026 course \emph{How To Biomanufacture
(Almost) Anything} (HTBAA) combines lectures, cloud-laboratory work, and student
projects in a global teaching network\cite{htbaa}. Its CFPS platform presents a
``Cell-Free Synthesis Coopetition for Humans \& AI,'' linking Ginkgo Bioworks
with HTBAA\cite{competition}.

An important predecessor is the OpenAI--Ginkgo collaboration reported by Smith
et al. in the February 2026 preprint \emph{Using a GPT-5-driven autonomous lab
to optimize the cost and titer of cell-free protein synthesis}\cite{smith}.
The study connected GPT-5 experimental proposals to validated robotic workflows
and returned measurements for subsequent design rounds. It reported a 40\%
reduction in specific protein-production cost and a 27\% increase in sfGFP titer
relative to its benchmark. Those results concern the tested system and reaction
geometry; they do not establish performance for other proteins or this competition.

The current coopetition makes formulation design a shared experimental problem.
Its interface supplies reagent stocks, literature formulations, reference recipes,
and control measurements for sfGFP, PETase (TfCut2 cutinase), and reteplase.
Participants work within fixed reaction and dispensing constraints. The readouts
also differ: sfGFP uses calibrated fluorescence, whereas the enzyme targets use
activity assays. A recipe can therefore affect measured performance through both
expression and target-specific folding or activity.

CFPS-Sim is our computational contribution to this setting. We propose to use
recipe accounting to enforce feasibility, mechanistic simulations to organize
resource hypotheses, and Bayesian optimization to choose informative experiments.
The scientific question is whether these calculations improve experimental
selection over a data-only baseline. The present executable scaffold establishes
the workflow; calibration and prospective experimental comparison remain future
milestones.

\subsection{Historical foundations: from translation biochemistry to engineering}
CFPS began as a way to dissect protein synthesis. Work with cell fractions in
the 1950s helped establish the roles of amino-acid activation and RNA carriers;
Hoagland, Zamecnik, and colleagues' 1958 study of a soluble RNA intermediate
is a foundational contribution to the discovery of transfer RNA\cite{hoagland}.
In 1961, Nirenberg and Matthaei used an \emph{E. coli} extract and synthetic
polyuridylic acid to direct polyphenylalanine synthesis, connecting an RNA
sequence to its amino-acid product and helping decipher the genetic
code\cite{nirenberg}. These studies established the principle that translation
could be investigated independently of intact cells.

The engineering problem subsequently became how to sustain that machinery.
Spirin et al.'s 1988 continuous-flow system supplied substrates and removed
product, extending translation for many hours\cite{spirin}. This work makes an
important modeling distinction visible: batch reactions and continuously fed
systems have different resource balances. In 2001, Shimizu et al. reconstituted
translation from purified components, establishing the PURE approach and a
route to controlling individual translation factors\cite{shimizu}. In contrast,
Jewett and Swartz's 2004 Cytomim work used physiologically motivated conditions
and pyruvate-powered metabolism to sustain efficient expression in crude
extract\cite{cytomim}.

These developments define two complementary traditions: lysate systems exploit
retained metabolic machinery, while reconstituted systems allow more explicit
control of composition. Our competition model follows the lysate tradition;
the POSTECH work below contributes ideas from the reconstituted tradition.
Resource supply, inhibitory byproducts, and active machinery are therefore
natural model variables, although their values depend on the experimental system.

\subsection{Modern entry points and influential experimental papers}
The following is a curated reading guide to modern CFPS, selected for breadth
and relevance to this project. It is not a verified ``most-read'' ranking:
publisher downloads, article views, and citations measure different things,
and no comparable readership dataset has been established here.

\begin{description}
\item[Gregorio, Levine, and Oza (2019).] \emph{A User's Guide to Cell-Free Protein
Synthesis}\cite{gregorio} is a practical entry point to choosing a platform,
preparing extracts, and setting up reactions. Read it before treating a published
recipe as portable across laboratories.
\item[Silverman, Karim, and Jewett (2020).] \emph{Cell-free gene expression: an
expanded repertoire of applications}\cite{silverman} connects CFPS to synthetic
biology, manufacturing, and prototyping. It supplies the broader motivation for
optimizing an open biochemical system.
\item[Hunt et al. (2025; online 2024).] \emph{Cell-Free Gene Expression: Methods
and Applications}\cite{hunt} is a comprehensive recent reference for extracts,
purified systems, energy chemistry, measurement, modeling, and applications.
Its discussion of reaction components provides a map for extending our modules.
\item[Pardee et al. (2014).] \emph{Paper-based synthetic gene networks}\cite{pardee}
demonstrated freeze-dried cell-free circuits on paper for sensing and diagnostics,
showing how CFPS can support deployable functions beyond bulk protein production.
\item[Kwon and Jewett (2015).] \emph{High-throughput preparation methods of crude
extract for robust cell-free protein synthesis}\cite{kwon} established accessible
sonication-based extract preparation. For this project, it highlights why lysate
preparation and batch identity belong in the metadata.
\end{description}

For computational development, pair these references with Horvath et al.'s
dynamic metabolic/expression model\cite{horvath}. For current experiment
selection, read the OpenAI--Ginkgo study\cite{smith} and Nishimori et al.'s
uncertainty-driven Bayesian optimization work\cite{nishimori}. These newer studies
are included for their direct relevance, without a claim about readership rank.

\subsection{POSTECH: modular optimization and the Build-a-Cell seminar}
The Build-a-Cell schedule lists ``Joon Lee,'' POSTECH, on August 31, 2026,
with the talk \emph{Automated, modular assembly of reconstituted cell-free
systems from in vitro-produced components}\cite{bac}. The corresponding paper
is by Chae et al., with Joongoo Lee as lead contact\cite{chae}. No recording
is linked in that schedule entry at the time of this documentation update;
the experimental account here is grounded in the paper, rather than a verified
transcript of the seminar.

The group's i-POPFLEX platform produces translation factors in lysate-based
CFPS, purifies them, and assembles a defined expression system. An Opentrons
OT-2 supports parallel component production and pooling, while automated FPLC
supports purification. This combines optimization of preparation time,
reproducibility, reagent cost, and biochemical function. The published study
reports preparation reduced from four days to two, with up to 8.4-fold higher
protein yield and 96\% lower reagent cost relative to its commercial-kit
comparison\cite{chae}. These are platform-specific benchmarks, not expected
improvements for our lysate competition.

The particularly useful diagnostic experiment exchanged functional groups
between in-vitro-produced and working in-vivo-produced preparations. The authors
localized loss of sfGFP expression to the energy-regeneration group. Producing
those components from redesigned linear DNA templates restored expression
\cite{chae,chae_preprint}. The intervention changed how the enzymes were made;
it was not simply a change in the final energy-substrate concentration.
The demonstrated workflow is experimental modular troubleshooting and platform
engineering; these results do not establish a Bayesian optimizer or a formal
meta-analysis of independent CFPS studies.

Our proposed adaptation is to exchange \emph{recipe modules}: salt/buffer,
energy/nucleotide, amino-acid, and folding/redox additions. Compare such exchanges
between feasible reference recipes, then examine interactions within the most
informative modules. Unlike i-POPFLEX, the competition fixes lysate containing
unmeasured endogenous machinery, so supplementation cannot reproduce the same
control over purified factors. In the simulator, module-specific sensitivity
and uncertainty analyses can propose hypotheses; in Bayesian optimization,
those hypotheses can guide candidate selection and exploratory conditions.
Replicated experiments must decide whether the proposed modules improve sfGFP
expression or transfer to enzyme activity. This is our design proposal, rather
than a result demonstrated by the POSTECH study.

\subsection{What measured biofoundry optimization has achieved}
Automated optimization has already produced substantial improvements, although
the readout and starting benchmark determine what each number means.
Borkowski et al. used acoustic liquid handling and active learning to explore
eleven buffer variables, measuring 1,017 compositions. They reported up to a
34-fold increase in relative sfGFP fluorescence yield compared with their initial
buffer for a homemade lysate, and identified a twenty-composition panel for
adapting predictions to other lysates\cite{borkowski}. The authors' affiliations
include French institutions and Manchester's SYNBIOCHEM; this CFPS yield result
should not be attributed to UIUC. It demonstrates experimentally measured scope
for formulation improvement, rather than a transferable fold-change target for
an already optimized competition reference.

Illinois supplies complementary examples of integrating automation with learning.
HamediRad et al.'s BioAutomata paired Bayesian optimization with UIUC's Illinois
Biological Foundry for Advanced Biomanufacturing (iBioFAB). Its demonstration
optimized cellular lycopene production, evaluated less than 1\% of the possible
variants, and outperformed random screening by 77\%\cite{bioautomata}.
More recently, Singh et al. integrated protein models, machine learning, and
iBioFAB automation in four rounds of enzyme engineering, reporting approximately
16-fold improvement in AtHMT ethyltransferase activity and 26-fold improvement
in YmPhytase activity at neutral pH\cite{singh}. Those gains describe activity
of engineered enzymes, not increased CFPS protein mass. Together, these studies
support the practical value of automated design--build--test--learn cycles while
keeping formulation yield, enzyme function, and metabolic titer distinct.

For CFPS-Sim, the direct formulation study motivates an empirical optimization
baseline and lysate-specific recalibration. The Illinois studies motivate
prospective comparisons against a defined alternative search policy and
confirmation using the actual functional readout. None provides an absolute
yield ceiling or validates our current kinetic constants. Report absolute target
readout, improvement over the same-plate competition reference, and uncertainty;
literature fold changes are context for what optimization has achieved.

\subsection{DNA design and the Popcorn connection}
DNA architecture is also relevant to CFPS performance. Promoter and untranslated
regions, the ribosome-binding site, coding sequence, and template format can
change transcription, translation initiation, RNA stability, and resource demand.
Modular CFPS plasmid development provides experimental precedent for treating
sequence context as part of expression-system design\cite{karim_plasmids}.
However, the competition's linked draft instructions specify supplied circular
plasmids driven by T7 expression, with fixed template additions\cite{competition_rules}.
Custom DNA is therefore not an established optimization variable in the current
submission scheme.

The existing \texttt{m9h/popcorn} toolkit offers a useful upstream design layer:
SBOL/GenBank representation, sequence-constrained codon optimization, RBS
estimates, assembly planning, and reaction-network export\cite{popcorn}.
Its current GFP example uses illustrative expression parameters and can fall
back to a random coding sequence; those outputs must not be treated as verified
competition constructs or sequence-calibrated predictions.

The implemented first integration uses versioned JSON and GenBank records for
the three supplied plasmids, with sequence checksums, template format, and source
provenance. Each CDS translates exactly to its reference protein in the draft
rules, including tags. This establishes consistency with the source, not
experimental identity. Coordinates are zero-based and half-open; schema version
1 supports contiguous forward-strand features. The transcript start is assumed
immediately after the upstream T7 promoter motif, and its end is assumed at the
supplied sequence boundary. Termination on a circular template is not established.
NTP counts therefore depend on assumed boundaries; amino-acid demands follow
the matched CDS. Sequence-based RBS and structure scores can become uncertain predictors
of initiation or stability, subject to calibration. Synonymous coding variants
retain amino-acid demand but can change RNA composition and structure. Keep
Popcorn and CFPS-Sim in separate environments and exchange versioned JSON,
GenBank artifacts. Import requires one CDS and explicit transcript bounds or one
mRNA annotation; it refuses to interpret a CDS as the complete transcript.
The importer also rejects an altered sequence whose embedded metadata retains
the original checksum, preventing silent replacement of a versioned construct.
The supplied constructs are characterized first;
explore new constructs as a separate research extension if the experimental
scope permits them.

<<construct_summary, echo=False, results='tex'>>=
from pathlib import Path
from cfps_sim.construct import read_construct, demands
print(r"\begin{center}\begin{tabular}{lrrr}Target & Plasmid bp & Protein aa & Assumed RNA nt\\\hline")
for target in ["sfgfp", "petase", "reteplase"]:
    stats = demands(read_construct(Path("../constructs/competition") / (target + ".json")))
    print(f"{target} & {stats['plasmid_bp']} & {stats['protein_aa']} & {stats['transcript_nt']}" + r"\\")
print(r"\end{tabular}\end{center}")
@

\begin{verbatim}
uv run cfps-sim construct inspect constructs/competition/sfgfp.json
uv run cfps-sim simulate \
  --construct constructs/competition/sfgfp.json --output results/sfgfp
\end{verbatim}
Inspection supports all three targets, including an optional static amino-acid
ceiling from user-supplied pools. It excludes endogenous pools, synthesis, and
recycling. Construct-aware simulation currently accepts sfGFP only; PETase and
reteplase require separate folding and activity models. The JSON and GenBank
exchange leaves Popcorn's dependencies and existing work separate from this
project. No RBS score, codon score, or DNA design is treated as a calibrated rate.

\section{Scope and evidence}
The project separates known additions, uncertain lysate contributions, simulated
predictions, and experimental observations. No demonstration output is evidence
of a productive competition recipe. Horvath et al. provide a starting point for
dynamic lysate modeling\cite{horvath}; Chae et al. motivate modular diagnostics
of cell-free systems\cite{chae}; Nishimori et al. motivate uncertainty-driven
experiment selection\cite{nishimori}. These papers inform the planned development;
the present numerical constants have not been extracted from those studies.

\section{Installation and execution}
The project uses Python 3.11 and a committed \texttt{uv.lock}. On macOS, install
\texttt{uv} and \texttt{tectonic} with Homebrew. From the repository root:
\begin{Verbatim}
uv sync --frozen --extra bo --extra cloud --extra docs
uv run cfps-sim validate examples/recipe.json
uv run cfps-sim simulate
uv run --extra bo cfps-sim bo-demo
uv run pytest
uv run --extra docs python scripts/build_docs.py
\end{Verbatim}
The final command weaves this article and compiles it with Tectonic.
Tectonic may download TeX support files on its first run.

\section{Recipe feasibility and units}
The saved platform constraints specify a $20{,}000$ nL reaction, transfers in
$25$ nL increments, and fixed lysate, base-buffer, and DNA volumes of
$5{,}000$, $2{,}000$, and $2{,}000$ nL. These constraints must be reconfirmed
before experimental submission. Remaining supplements and water occupy
$11{,}000$ nL. For a molar stock,
\[
C_{i,\mathrm{known}}=C_{i,\mathrm{stock}}\frac{V_i}{V_{\mathrm{reaction}}}
+ C_{i,\mathrm{base,final}}.
\]
Base contributions must be supplied already diluted. Endogenous lysate pools are
excluded. Mass concentrations, activity units, mixed stocks, and volume fractions
require explicit chemical mappings before they can enter a molar model.

<<recipe, echo=False>>=
import json
from pathlib import Path
from cfps_sim.recipe import validate
root = Path.cwd().parent
recipe = json.loads((root / "examples/recipe.json").read_text())
checked = validate(recipe)
print("Total volume (nL):", sum(checked["volumes_nl"].values()))
print("Supplement magnesium glutamate (mM):", checked["supplement_mM"]["magnesium_glutamate"])
print("Water (nL):", checked["volumes_nl"]["nuclease_free_water"])
